      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find the degree measure of an angle whose measure is double the measure of its complement, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Define the angle and its complement:**\n   Let the angle be \\( x \\) degrees. The complement of this angle is \\( 90^\\circ - x \\).\n\n2. **Set up the relationship:**\n   According to the problem, the angle is double the measure of its complement. This gives us the equation:\n   \\[\n   x = 2(90^\\circ - x)\n   \\]\n\n3. **Solve the equation:**\n   First, expand the right-hand side:\n   \\[\n   x = 180^\\circ - 2x\n   \\]\n   Next, add \\( 2x \\) to both sides to isolate \\( x \\):\n   \\[\n   x + 2x = 180^\\circ\n   \\]\n   Simplify:\n   \\[\n   3x = 180^\\circ\n   \\]\n   Finally, divide both sides by 3:\n   \\[\n   x = 60^\\circ\n   \\]\n\n4. **Verify the solution:**\n   The complement of \\( 60^\\circ \\) is \\( 90^\\circ - 60^\\circ = 30^\\circ \\). Check if \\( 60^\\circ \\) is double the measure of its complement:\n   \\[\n   60^\\circ = 2 \\times 30^\\circ\n   \\]\n   This is true, so our solution is correct.\n\nTherefore, the degree measure of the angle is \\(\\boxed{60}\\).",
